      howItWorks: "1. You declare `type name;` and optionally assign `= value;` on the same line.\n2. Primitives (byte, short, int, long, float, double, char, boolean) are stored by value — assigning one copies the bits.\n3. References point at heap objects; assigning a reference copies the pointer, not the object.\n4. Literal forms tell the compiler the type: `100L` is a long, `3.14f` is a float, `'A'` is a char, `true` is a boolean.\n5. `var` (Java 10+) lets the compiler infer the type from the right-hand side — still statically typed, just less to write.",
