      definition: "Operators perform computations on values. Java provides arithmetic (+, -, *, /, %), relational (==, !=, <, >, <=, >=), logical (&&, ||, !), assignment (=, +=, -=), bitwise (&, |, ^, <<, >>), and the ternary operator (?:). Operator precedence and associativity determine evaluation order; use parentheses to make intent explicit.",
      coreUnderstanding: "Java operators are symbols that perform operations on operands. The main categories: arithmetic (+, -, *, /, %), relational (==, !=, <, >, <=, >=), logical (&&, ||, !), bitwise (&, |, ^, ~, <<, >>), assignment (=, +=, -=, *=), and the ternary (?:). Short-circuit evaluation: && and || evaluate the right operand only if needed. Integer division truncates toward zero (7/2 = 3, -7/2 = -3). The ternary operator is a compact if-else expression: condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse.",
      howItWorks: "1. Arithmetic: +, -, *, /, %. Integer division truncates: 7/2 = 3, 7%2 = 1.\n2. Relational: ==, !=, <, >, <=, >=. Return boolean.\n3. Logical: && (AND, short-circuit), || (OR, short-circuit), ! (NOT). Short-circuit: if left is false, right is not evaluated (for &&).\n4. Bitwise: &, |, ^, ~, <<, >>, >>>. Operate on bits. >> is arithmetic shift (preserves sign), >>> is logical shift (fills with 0).\n5. Assignment: =, +=, -=, *=, /=, %=. x += 5 is shorthand for x = x + 5.\n6. Ternary: int max = (a > b) ? a : b. An expression, not a statement.\n7. Precedence: * before +, && before ||. Use parentheses for clarity.",
      codeExamples: [{ lang: "java", code: "public class Demo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        // Arithmetic\n        int a = 17, b = 5;\n        System.out.println(a / b);   // 3 (integer division)\n        System.out.println(a % b);   // 2 (remainder)\n        System.out.println(a / 5.0); // 3.4 (double division)\n\n        // Logical (short-circuit)\n        int x = 0;\n        if (x != 0 && 10 / x > 1) {  // x != 0 is false -> right not evaluated!\n            System.out.println(\"won't print\");\n        }\n\n        // Ternary\n        int max = (a > b) ? a : b;\n        System.out.println(max);  // 17\n\n        // Bitwise\n        int mask = 0b1111;\n        int val = 0b1010;\n        System.out.println(val & mask);  // 10 (AND)\n        System.out.println(val | 0b0101); // 15 (OR)\n        System.out.println(1 << 4);       // 16 (left shift = *2^4)\n\n        // Compound assignment\n        int score = 100;\n        score += 50;  // score = score + 50\n        System.out.println(score);  // 150\n    }\n}", output: "3\n2\n3.4\n17\n10\n15\n16\n150", note: "Integer division truncates (17/5 = 3). && and || short-circuit (skip right operand if result is determined). Ternary is a compact if-else expression." }],
