      coreUnderstanding: "Console I/O in Java is done through System.out (printing) and Scanner (reading). System.out.println prints a line of text. System.out.printf provides formatted output (like C's printf) with format specifiers: %d (int), %f (double), %s (string), %n (newline). Scanner reads input from the keyboard (System.in) or a file. It provides methods for different types: nextInt(), nextDouble(), nextBoolean(), nextLine(). The nextLine() pitfall: after nextInt(), the newline character remains in the buffer, so nextLine() returns an empty string. Call an extra nextLine() to consume it.",
      howItWorks: "1. System.out.println(x): prints x followed by a newline.\n2. System.out.print(x): prints without newline.\n3. System.out.printf(format, args...): formatted output. printf(\"%s is %d years old%n\", name, age).\n4. Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in): creates a Scanner for keyboard input.\n5. scanner.nextInt(): reads an int. scanner.nextDouble(): reads a double. scanner.next(): reads a word (until whitespace).\n6. scanner.nextLine(): reads the entire line. Pitfall: after nextInt(), call nextLine() to consume the leftover newline.\n7. scanner.close(): closes the Scanner. Never close one wrapping System.in — it closes stdin for the whole program.\n8. Format specifiers: %d (int), %f (double), %s (string), %c (char), %b (boolean), %n (newline), %% (literal %).",
      codeExamples: [{ lang: "java", code: "import java.util.Scanner;\n\npublic class Demo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n\n        // Reading input\n        System.out.print(\"Enter your name: \");\n        String name = sc.nextLine();\n\n        System.out.print(\"Enter your age: \");\n        int age = sc.nextInt();\n\n        // Formatted output\n        System.out.printf(\"Hello %s, you are %d years old.%n\", name, age);\n        System.out.printf(\"Next year: %d%n\", age + 1);\n        System.out.printf(\"PI is approximately %.2f%n\", Math.PI);\n\n        // Common pitfall: nextLine() after nextInt()\n        // sc.nextInt() reads 30 but leaves \"\\n\" in the buffer\n        // sc.nextLine() would return \"\" (empty) without the extra call\n        sc.nextLine();  // consume the leftover newline\n        System.out.print(\"Enter your city: \");\n        String city = sc.nextLine();\n        System.out.printf(\"City: %s%n\", city);\n\n        // Do NOT close sc if it wraps System.in\n        // sc.close();  // would close stdin!\n    }\n}", output: "(interactive input/output)", note: "Scanner reads input (nextInt, nextLine). printf provides formatted output. The nextLine()-after-nextInt() pitfall: call an extra nextLine() to consume the leftover newline." }],
